WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:09.832
So hi everybody, my name is Michael Rosbash,

00:00:09.833 --> 00:00:11.999
and I'm an investigator of the Howard Hughes Medical

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:15.632
Institute, and a professor at Brandeis University in the department

00:00:15.633 --> 00:00:19.832
of biology, where I've been for 40 years. So, the friendship

00:00:19.833 --> 00:00:23.766
aspect of this title refers to the fact that I collaborated for

00:00:23.767 --> 00:00:26.232
more than 20 years with my colleague, Jeff Hall,

00:00:26.233 --> 00:00:28.932
who arrived at Brandeis at exactly the same time

00:00:28.933 --> 00:00:33.499
I did. And we formed a fast friendship. Jeff is almost exactly

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:37.099
my age, and we had many interests in common. And

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:40.899
Jeff had worked as a postdoc for Seymour Benzer,

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:44.699
where he met Ron Konopka, where he became fluent

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:49.499
with the circadian story and also worked on that himself

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:52.832
in his own lab, before I got involved. So that's really how

00:00:52.833 --> 00:00:56.699
this story began. And Jeff and I, as I indicated, had many

00:00:56.700 --> 00:01:00.666
interests in common. One -- two of them are shown here,

00:01:00.667 --> 00:01:05.332
tobacco and alcohol. We also both were great sports fans,

00:01:05.333 --> 00:01:09.132
and also played basketball for a decade together in a

00:01:09.133 --> 00:01:13.032
religious-obsession with the noon-time basketball game

00:01:13.033 --> 00:01:15.999
during that decade. And it was after the basketball games I

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:18.399
in the locker room where everyone cools down and showers

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:21.132
that we would exchange scientific stories. And Jeff

00:01:21.133 --> 00:01:24.866
would tell me about his research in circadian rhythms that was

00:01:24.867 --> 00:01:29.732
ongoing. And it's really that friendship and those tales

00:01:29.733 --> 00:01:33.699
which were the backdrop for this story. So to tell you

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:37.066
just a little bit about circadian biology, as shown here

00:01:37.067 --> 00:01:40.766
is the wheel running activity of hamsters recorded

00:01:40.767 --> 00:01:44.266
on an old machine and then plotted as a function of time

00:01:44.267 --> 00:01:50.066
of day, from the top to the bottom. And all animals have some

00:01:50.067 --> 00:01:54.732
circadian rhythms, from fungi to man. And they're commonly

00:01:54.733 --> 00:01:58.566
assayed in constant darkness. And the rhythms, the

00:01:58.567 --> 00:02:01.866
intrinsic rhythms of animals, are not precisely 24 hours

00:02:01.867 --> 00:02:05.199
-- they're about 24 hours, a little slower, a little faster.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:08.332
Hence, the term "circadian" or "circa dia," about a day.

00:02:08.333 --> 00:02:13.166
And shown here, is the rhythm of these rodents in constant

00:02:13.167 --> 00:02:17.099
darkness, which is a little faster than 24 hours, as indicated by

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:20.366
every day starts a little bit earlier than the day before.

00:02:20.367 --> 00:02:24.966
And in a light-dark cycle, however, shown at the bottom

00:02:24.967 --> 00:02:28.832
panel, where there's 12 hours of light and 12 hours of darkness,

00:02:28.833 --> 00:02:32.966
then the animal locks on to the light-dark cycle in the incubator

00:02:32.967 --> 00:02:37.499
as we lock onto a light-dark cycle from the external world.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:41.499
The set rising and setting of the sun, and temperature cycles

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:45.066
can also do this as they accompany the light-dark in the external

00:02:45.067 --> 00:02:49.099
light-dark cycle. You'll notice here also, that these animals

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:52.332
are nocturnal, their activity coincides with the night, they

00:02:52.333 --> 00:02:56.199
sleep during the daytime, and that's a characteristic of any

00:02:56.200 --> 00:03:01.399
but not all rodents. Now the star of our show is Drosophila

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:05.532
melanogaster, as shown here using a primitive counting

00:03:05.533 --> 00:03:09.866
apparatus to keep time. This is drawn by my daughter, who is

00:03:09.867 --> 00:03:16.866
a graphic designer. And here's a picture of Drosophila

00:03:16.867 --> 00:03:20.832
locomotor activity rhythms, the analog if you like, of the

00:03:20.833 --> 00:03:24.432
hamster's wheel running activity. And what's shown are the

00:03:24.433 --> 00:03:28.032
fact that these animals, insects -- most insects, in general,

00:03:28.033 --> 00:03:31.999
are active at dawn and dusk, with a morning peak

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:34.599
and an evening peak of activity. In this case, in an

00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:39.532
incubator, so the morning peak coincides with the appearance

00:03:39.533 --> 00:03:43.032
of light, whereas the evening peak coincides with the

00:03:43.033 --> 00:03:46.766
transition from light to dark. And the arrows refer to

00:03:46.767 --> 00:03:50.832
the fact that the activity ramps up in advance of those

00:03:50.833 --> 00:03:54.432
discontinuous incubator transitions, indicating that there's

00:03:54.433 --> 00:03:58.232
really a timing mechanism that can anticipate the change

00:03:58.233 --> 00:04:01.699
in the external world's conditions. And anticipation

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:04.166
is the name of the game in circadian rhythms. It's

00:04:04.167 --> 00:04:06.999
more advantageous to know what's going to happen

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:10.899
than to know what has just happened. And so that's

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:16.732
really what the circadian system contributes to the animals,

00:04:16.733 --> 00:04:21.199
one of several things. So genetics, in terms of addressing

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:24.599
this kind of problem, has had some negative history, especially

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:29.032
in the first half of the 20th century. For example, the eugenics

00:04:29.033 --> 00:04:34.732
movement, in which people of low intelligence, handicapped,

00:04:34.733 --> 00:04:39.266
or even of some disfavored races at the time, were

00:04:39.267 --> 00:04:43.599
inhibited from procreating, in some cases exterminated

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:47.032
as you all know from Nazi Germany. So this movement

00:04:47.033 --> 00:04:51.599
gave behavioral genetics, gave intrinsic properties of

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:57.999
animals or people, a bad name. And similarly, the psychologist

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:01.532
B.F. Skinner, who had a long and distinguished career at Harvard,

00:05:01.533 --> 00:05:04.699
had a tremendous amount of influence and believed that one

00:05:04.700 --> 00:05:08.066
could change the behavior of virtually every individual

00:05:08.067 --> 00:05:11.632
by simply subjecting them to constraining conditions,

00:05:11.633 --> 00:05:15.466
which would then change their behavior. Meaning

00:05:15.467 --> 00:05:20.832
that intrinsic behavioral features were not really, were not really

00:05:20.833 --> 00:05:26.199
fixed. And so, of course there's positive history, especially

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:29.766
in the latter half of the 20th century that offsets that preceding

00:05:29.767 --> 00:05:32.899
negative history. We know that we can breed plants

00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:37.599
in order to enhance certain characteristics, that's also

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:42.099
true for animals, cattle, dogs, etc ... And of course,

00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:46.099
the 1973 Nobel Prize was awarded to three scientists

00:05:46.100 --> 00:05:51.666
who really worked on animal behavior as intrinsic

00:05:51.667 --> 00:05:56.299
to those particular species. So the landscape shifted,

00:05:56.300 --> 00:05:59.266
I would say, as the 20th century progressed.

00:05:59.267 --> 00:06:03.299
And so, the question for you and for me, is

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:06.366
why use genetics to try and address the problem like

00:06:06.367 --> 00:06:09.266
circadian rhythms? And I want to emphasize that this is

00:06:09.267 --> 00:06:11.966
different from the nature/nurture problem, we're not

00:06:11.967 --> 00:06:15.866
trying to debate how much of circadian biology is inherited,

00:06:15.867 --> 00:06:18.832
and how much can be environmental. Nor whether

00:06:18.833 --> 00:06:21.832
you're rhythm is different from my rhythm because of genetic

00:06:21.833 --> 00:06:25.499
features or environmental features. We're using genetics

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:29.366
as an entree into this process. Can we figure out what

00:06:29.367 --> 00:06:33.732
keeps time by finding mutants which disrupt that process?

00:06:33.733 --> 00:06:37.866
And therefore will provide access to the genes and the proteins

00:06:37.867 --> 00:06:42.932
which underlie the process. And to emphasize this distinction,

00:06:42.933 --> 00:06:45.866
let me point out to you that even learning, the ultimate

00:06:45.867 --> 00:06:51.866
in nurture, if you like, relies on proteins and therefore,

00:06:51.867 --> 00:06:55.899
relies on genes. So genetics, genes, proteins are

00:06:55.900 --> 00:07:00.632
inherent in everything. So I turn now to the studies of Konopka

00:07:00.633 --> 00:07:05.266
and Benzer, who identified clock mutants of Drosophila

00:07:05.267 --> 00:07:09.832
melanogaster in this landmark paper published in 1971.

00:07:09.833 --> 00:07:14.832
So they fed fruit flies mutagens to increase frequency

00:07:14.833 --> 00:07:19.832
of mutations and then screened those flies for aberrant

00:07:19.833 --> 00:07:23.632
circadian properties. And they came up with three mutants

00:07:23.633 --> 00:07:28.299
in single gene, all three were alleles of one gene,

00:07:28.300 --> 00:07:33.799
which either gave rise to an absent clock, that is no

00:07:33.800 --> 00:07:37.132
rhythm what so ever, a short period clock, which ran

00:07:37.133 --> 00:07:41.532
fast, or a long period clock, which ran slow. And all

00:07:41.533 --> 00:07:45.466
three of those, as I said, were single mutations, single

00:07:45.467 --> 00:07:48.932
nucleotide changes in the same protein, which they named

00:07:48.933 --> 00:07:52.266
Period, which suggested of course that this protein had some

00:07:52.267 --> 00:07:56.066
important role in the timekeeping process. But it was really

00:07:56.067 --> 00:08:01.999
-- and here's an example of the wild type circadian pattern

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:06.366
on the left, or the per short pattern on the right. And the

00:08:06.367 --> 00:08:09.266
vertical line refers to the fact that the lights were turned

00:08:09.267 --> 00:08:12.599
off. And you'll notice that the wild type fly, on your left,

00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:18.032
keeps very good time during the course of the subsequent

00:08:18.033 --> 00:08:22.232
days, that is almost exactly 24 hours. Whereas the per short

00:08:22.233 --> 00:08:26.832
mutant strain, every day advances the period by several

00:08:26.833 --> 00:08:29.232
-- advances the beginning of activity by several hours

00:08:29.233 --> 00:08:32.232
hours by comparison to the day before. Very similar to

00:08:32.233 --> 00:08:35.166
the hamster phenotype, as I said before, only more

00:08:35.167 --> 00:08:39.399
profound, since this animal has a 19 or 20 hour period

00:08:39.400 --> 00:08:43.166
as contrasted with the almost precise 24 hour period

00:08:43.167 --> 00:08:46.699
of the wild type. So, how does one get at this problem?

00:08:46.700 --> 00:08:51.666
And there was really almost a 15 years between the

00:08:51.667 --> 00:08:55.799
publication of Konopka and Benzer and the appearance of

00:08:55.800 --> 00:08:59.566
recombinant DNA, which could be applied to this problem

00:08:59.567 --> 00:09:02.632
where the Period gene could be cloned and sequenced

00:09:02.633 --> 00:09:05.866
to try to ask what might this gene be doing?

00:09:05.867 --> 00:09:09.232
And so I was working on recombinant DNA in my laboratory

00:09:09.233 --> 00:09:13.966
on studies on yeast gene expression, Jeff Hall had not

00:09:13.967 --> 00:09:16.566
yet adopted this technology. So during one of these

00:09:16.567 --> 00:09:19.399
basketball games, I suggested to Jeff that we get

00:09:19.400 --> 00:09:22.466
together, that we collaborate and that we try to clone

00:09:22.467 --> 00:09:24.866
this gene, our two laboratories, to see if we could

00:09:24.867 --> 00:09:29.166
figure out what it was doing. And we did that at the same time

00:09:29.167 --> 00:09:32.632
Mike Young's laboratory at the Rockefeller University also

00:09:32.633 --> 00:09:35.999
accomplished the same task. And unfortunately, this protein

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:39.632
was a pioneer protein, as the expression goes, it

00:09:39.633 --> 00:09:42.432
had no relatives in the database. These were early days

00:09:42.433 --> 00:09:45.766
for DNA sequencing, there were a limited number of proteins

00:09:45.767 --> 00:09:48.699
that had been sequenced and identified, and so we

00:09:48.700 --> 00:09:51.399
were left still with the question of what this protein

00:09:51.400 --> 00:09:57.732
did. And it was Paul Hardin in his explorations of the Period

00:09:57.733 --> 00:10:01.199
gene's expression, where he discovered that the Period

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:04.899
messenger RNA underwent strong circadian oscillations

00:10:04.900 --> 00:10:09.199
in time, so messenger RNA levels increased and decreased

00:10:09.200 --> 00:10:12.766
by about a factor of 10. With a precise 24 hour period

00:10:12.767 --> 00:10:16.532
even in constant darkness, and remarkably, shown in

00:10:16.533 --> 00:10:22.232
blue, the per short messenger RNA, different, I remind you

00:10:22.233 --> 00:10:26.366
by only one single nucleotide from the wild type or normal

00:10:26.367 --> 00:10:30.366
strain, had only 20 hours between their peaks, just like

00:10:30.367 --> 00:10:35.566
its behavior as compared to this 24 hour difference in

00:10:35.567 --> 00:10:38.766
the wild type. So the RNA reflected the behavior and

00:10:38.767 --> 00:10:42.766
we proposed that the protein must feed back onto its

00:10:42.767 --> 00:10:46.499
messenger RNA somehow, and that that was intrinsic

00:10:46.500 --> 00:10:49.232
and important for the timing process. But we didn't know

00:10:49.233 --> 00:10:52.466
at what level this occurred. We didn't know whether this

00:10:52.467 --> 00:10:57.332
was transcriptional in origin, yet the focus on gene expression

00:10:57.333 --> 00:11:00.532
was something I was very familiar with and knew that the

00:11:00.533 --> 00:11:05.799
half lives of mRNAs in higher organisms were generally

00:11:05.800 --> 00:11:09.366
in the hours or even tens of hours range. Something that

00:11:09.367 --> 00:11:13.332
was perfect for being related to circadian timing.

00:11:13.333 --> 00:11:16.732
And then a publication in 1988 by Steve Crews and

00:11:16.733 --> 00:11:19.932
colleagues identified a Drosophila transcription factor

00:11:19.933 --> 00:11:23.432
named Single Minded, which had homology with the period

00:11:23.433 --> 00:11:29.932
protein. And that set us off on an expedition to rapidly

00:11:29.933 --> 00:11:33.066
see if transcription was part of the story.

00:11:33.067 --> 00:11:35.599
And sure enough, within the next two years, we were able

00:11:35.600 --> 00:11:39.166
to show through several independent kinds of investigation,

00:11:39.167 --> 00:11:43.432
that the Period protein was nuclear and it affected transcription

00:11:43.433 --> 00:11:46.366
and therefore, we could propose that the feedback

00:11:46.367 --> 00:11:50.566
loop was direct and was transcriptional on origin.

00:11:50.567 --> 00:11:54.432
And over the subsequent six years or so, the circadian

00:11:54.433 --> 00:11:59.266
field really exploded. And both identified the positive transcription

00:11:59.267 --> 00:12:03.532
factor, responsible for the synthesis of these regulators,

00:12:03.533 --> 00:12:08.332
led by Takahashi and his colleagues in mammalian studies.

00:12:08.333 --> 00:12:12.199
Followed very quickly by genetic studies in my lab and

00:12:12.200 --> 00:12:16.766
others. And in addition, people made the leap between flies

00:12:16.767 --> 00:12:19.466
and mammals, showing for example, that the Period protein

00:12:19.467 --> 00:12:25.132
was conserved and had a relative, a very close relative in mammals

00:12:25.133 --> 00:12:29.666
which did very similar, if not identical job. So there was

00:12:29.667 --> 00:12:33.666
really a grand synthesis, if you'd like, and this story

00:12:33.667 --> 00:12:41.499
has held up for the 17 years since 1998. And so

00:12:41.500 --> 00:12:45.366
the question, the summary if you will, is what do I draw

00:12:45.367 --> 00:12:49.899
as a personal conclusion from this journey of mine?

00:12:49.900 --> 00:12:52.966
And I would say, both from the nature and nurture

00:12:52.967 --> 00:12:57.632
standpoint, I've been incredibly lucky, from the nurture

00:12:57.633 --> 00:13:00.366
point of view, I went to Brandeis almost by accident.

00:13:00.367 --> 00:13:04.332
It was not a very well-considered decision, and Brandeis

00:13:04.333 --> 00:13:07.599
has been a wonderful place to do work. I've had great

00:13:07.600 --> 00:13:11.832
people who have come to my lab. I met Jeff Hall, to collaborate

00:13:11.833 --> 00:13:15.332
and my colleagues in the administration have been remarkably

00:13:15.333 --> 00:13:20.799
supportive and allowed me to undertake this thrilling

00:13:20.800 --> 00:13:28.266
enterprise. The NIH and in particular, HHMI, which has

00:13:28.267 --> 00:13:32.832
funded me for the last 25 years, have really been a wonderful

00:13:32.833 --> 00:13:35.932
source of support. HHMI funds people and not projects.

00:13:35.933 --> 00:13:39.566
Meaning I could more or less do whatever I wanted.

00:13:39.567 --> 00:13:42.632
And was able to accomplish this. I've had a very supportive

00:13:42.633 --> 00:13:48.232
family, that despite my obsessions loved me and allowed me to

00:13:48.233 --> 00:13:51.966
pursue this. And finally, let me emphasize that even from

00:13:51.967 --> 00:13:55.366
the nature point of view, whatever talents we individually

00:13:55.367 --> 00:13:59.132
bring to a problem, those talents, what we inherit from our

00:13:59.133 --> 00:14:03.432
parents, as we say, those parents are not -- those genes are not

00:14:03.433 --> 00:14:08.166
by choice, we are all victims of accident. In my case,

00:14:08.167 --> 00:14:14.032
a happy victim. And so it's really good fortune that underlies

00:14:14.033 --> 00:14:15.933
this story. Thank you very much.

